{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "Classification of hardware-related incidents by hardware problem type",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "hardware_problem_type",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Directly decomposes hardware incidents into a coherent typology (Printer, Monitor, Keyboard, Storage, CPU/GPU, etc.), which is the core of the implied focus and directly answers the query about kinds of hardware problems."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_hardware_incident",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "A gating flag that scopes the population to hardware incidents; coherent with the focus and explains the NULLs in the type column, though it is a coarse binary restatement rather than an added facet."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.9,
  "structure_quality": 0.55,
  "focus_actionability": 0.85,
  "rationale": "Both columns center tightly on one data-inferable focus: hardware problem classification. focus_coherence is high because there is no grab-bag; both columns serve the same concept. However, structure_quality is only moderate: the two columns are effectively one categorical typology plus its own binary derivative (is_hardware_incident is entailed by hardware_problem_type being non-null), so the analytical structure is thin -- it captures the 'what type' facet but adds no orthogonal facets (e.g., severity/component/root-cause/resolution) to build a richer structure around the focus. The typology itself is at a useful granularity, avoiding both single-restatement and noise, which keeps it above mid. focus_actionability is high because a hardware-problem-type breakdown is directly investigable (e.g., prioritizing printer vs. display issues)."
}

